id: transfiguration
name: Transfiguration
type: standard
actionCost: 2
kiCostPerTier: 10
attacking: false
requiresTarget: true
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
transfiguration: true
exploitable: >
  All adjacent Opponents. If you are knocked through a Health Threshold due to an
  Attacking Maneuver from the Exploit Maneuver in response to this Maneuver, you
  automatically fail the Strike Roll for this Maneuver's effects.
resource: anitem
resourceLabel: An Item
resourceMax: 1
resourceInternal: true
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Turn an Opponent in your Melee Range into an object. A Clash of your Strike against
  their Strike or Dodge leaves them Transfigured for the Encounter; a Might Clash after it
  makes them the Item outright, unable to act - and destroying that Item kills them.
text: >
  Transfiguration Maneuver [1/Round]: Transform your target into an object of some kind.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard Maneuver
  –Action Cost: 2 Actions
  –Minions: Non-Minion
  –KP Cost: 10(T)
  –Exploitable: All adjacent Opponents. If you are knocked through a Health Threshold due
  to an Attacking Maneuver from the Exploit Maneuver in response to this Maneuver, you
  automatically fail the Strike Roll for this Maneuver’s effects.
  –Effect: Make a Clash (Physical Strike vs Strike/Dodge) against an Opponent within your
  Melee Range. If you win, choose an Item – your Opponent becomes that Item until the end
  of the Combat Encounter and has their Size Category changed to suit this Item (your ARC
  decides). This Opponent is Transfigured (see — Transfigured).

  After your Opponent becomes an Item, make a Might Clash against that Opponent, though
  if that Opponent’s Tier of Power is higher than yours, reduce your Dice Score for this
  Clash by 1(T). If you win this second Clash, the Transfigured Opponent, for all intents
  and purposes, becomes that Item and cannot make any type of Maneuver until the end of
  the Combat Encounter (after which, if they are still alive, they return to normal). If
  that Item would be destroyed or used up (such as a Snack), that Character dies. They
  must spend a Karma Point to avoid dying and have their body appear in the closest
  unoccupied Square. They are still Defeated.

  If you target a Character turned into an Item with the Transfiguration Maneuver, turn
  them back to normal.

  A Character targeted by this Maneuver may spend 1 Counter Action. If they do, and they
  beat the initial Clash for the effects of the Transfiguration Maneuver, then you must
  change the target of this use of the Transfiguration Maneuver to yourself and make the
  rolls as if you were targeted by another Character (all rolls involved become Urgent).

---

# --- Two Clashes, in sequence rather than by choice -----------------------------------------
#
# The Thrust's shape, and the second Maneuver here to have it: win the first and a second
# one follows. What differs is that the Thrust's second Clash is one of two effects a player
# chooses between, and this one simply comes next - "after your Opponent becomes an Item,
# make a Might Clash against that Opponent". Nothing is chosen, so nothing is asked.
#
# A tie goes to the Defender in both, here as everywhere else.
#
# `transfiguration: true` rather than a script, for the reason the Thrust gives: a script
# can neither open a Clash nor wait for one to land.
#
# --- "Physical Strike" -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is one Strike Roll in this system. A Foundation decides which Damage Attribute the
# Wound Roll reads and nothing about Strike, so "Physical Strike" names the kind of attack
# this stands in for rather than a second Strike value to look up.
#
# Built as the Strike Roll, which is also what the Grapple Check and the Thrust use for
# "(Strike vs Strike/Dodge)". If a per-Foundation Strike ever exists, this is the line that
# would want changing, and it is the only one.
#
# The slash on the other side is a choice made by whoever is rolling it, as it is in every
# other "Strike/Dodge" here.
#
# --- The Item ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Choose an Item." There are no objects in this system - no equipment, no inventory, and a
# Foundry Item here is a Maneuver or a Talent - so what is chosen is a name, typed by the
# player who won the Clash.
#
# Asked on the card rather than when the Maneuver is used, because the entry asks it after
# the Clash is won: naming a teacup for a Clash you then lose is a question that should not
# have been put.
#
# Kept on the character, not only on the card. "For all intents and purposes, becomes that
# Item" lasts the rest of the Encounter, and the card that said which Item scrolls away
# inside a round.
#
# "Has their Size Category changed to suit this Item (your ARC decides)." The ARC decides,
# so nothing here does. Said on the card, with the Slot that would change a Size Category
# named, so whoever is running it knows where to put the answer if they want it recorded.
#
# --- The Might Clash's penalty ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If that Opponent's Tier of Power is higher than yours, reduce your Dice Score for this
# Clash by 1(T)." The second rule in these rules to set the two sides' Tiers against each
# other - the Terrify's -2 is the first, and this one is scaled where that one is flat.
#
# Yours, not theirs: a row on the challenger's side. Strictly higher, so equal Tiers are
# not higher. And 1(T) of the challenger's own Tier of Power, which is what (T) means on a
# number that belongs to whoever is rolling it.
#
# --- Becoming the Item outright ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Cannot make any type of Maneuver until the end of the Combat Encounter."
#
# `forbid maneuvers` is the Slot for that and it is already read at the door - it is what
# Sleeping and Slowed at three stacks write. What it needed was somewhere to be written
# from, on a clock nobody else's could take off, so it is held as a Resource with an
# Encounter clock and read by the passive below.
#
# The passive is in this file and runs on everybody, which is how the Blockade does the
# same trick: every character carries a copy of the Core Maneuvers, so a Core Maneuver's
# passive is the one place a rule can be read on the character it binds. The character
# being turned into a teacup does not own the Transfiguration that did it - what they own
# is their own copy of it.
#
# Not folded into the Transfigured Combat Condition. Transfigured is what losing the FIRST
# Clash leaves, and a Transfigured Character still fights: its own entry gives them -2(bT)
# on Combat Rolls and Physical attacks only, which is a rule about somebody who can still
# act. Only the second Clash stops them.
#
# "After which, if they are still alive, they return to normal." The Encounter clock is the
# whole of that: it comes off when the Encounter ends, and the Transfigured Condition is on
# the same clock. "If they are still alive" needs nothing - a dead Character is not brought
# back by a clock running out.
#
# --- Dying as an Item ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If that Item would be destroyed or used up (such as a Snack), that Character dies. They
# must spend a Karma Point to avoid dying and have their body appear in the closest
# unoccupied Square. They are still Defeated."
#
# Nothing here can know that a teacup was broken, so it is a button on the settled card
# rather than something watched for. Pressed by whoever is running the fight.
#
# What follows is built: they are Defeated either way, and the Karma Point is offered to
# whoever owns them rather than spent for them - "they MUST spend a Karma Point to avoid
# dying" is a choice with a price, and a system that spends somebody's last Karma Point
# without asking has made the choice for them.
#
# "Have their body appear in the closest unoccupied Square" is said and not done, like
# every Square here.
#
# Read carefully, "that Character dies" and "they are still Defeated" are not the same
# outcome twice: the Karma Point buys you out of dying and not out of being Defeated. So
# the button leads to Defeated in both branches and the difference is whether they are also
# dead - which this system records as a defeat that was not bought out of, because it has
# no separate state for dead. Said on the card in those words.
#
# --- Turning them back -------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If you target a Character turned into an Item with the Transfiguration Maneuver, turn
# them back to normal."
#
# A branch before the Clash: no Clash, no Item named, nothing rolled. It takes the Actions
# and the Ki like any other use, because the entry describes a use of the Maneuver and
# takes nothing off its cost.
#
# "A Character turned into an Item" is read as one who has been through the second Clash -
# the one who is the Item - rather than merely Transfigured. That is what the phrase names
# three paragraphs earlier, and it is also the only reading where this is worth an Action:
# a merely Transfigured Character can be un-Transfigured by anything that removes a Combat
# Condition.
#
# Whose Transfiguration it was does not matter. The entry says "a Character turned into an
# Item", not one you turned - so anybody with this Maneuver can undo anybody's.
#
# --- The Counter Action ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "A Character targeted by this Maneuver may spend 1 Counter Action. If they do, and they
# beat the initial Clash for the effects of the Transfiguration Maneuver, then you must
# change the target of this use of the Transfiguration Maneuver to yourself and make the
# rolls as if you were targeted by another Character (all rolls involved become Urgent)."
#
# Offered on the first Clash's card, to the side it was aimed at, while the Clash is still
# open - the Counter Action has to be spent before the rolls, since it is what decides what
# beating them does.
#
# "Beat" is beating, not tying. A tie goes to the Defender everywhere here, so a tie is
# already theirs and the Maneuver already fails - what the Counter Action buys is the part
# beyond that, and the entry asks for a beat by name.
#
# "Make the rolls as if you were targeted by another Character." So the Maneuver stays
# yours and you are its target: your Strike against your own Strike or Dodge, both sides
# rolled by you. Which is what the card does - one character on both sides of a Clash,
# which nothing here had done before and which the machinery took without changes.
#
# "All rolls involved become Urgent" - so neither of your own rolls can be failed on
# purpose to get out of it. Urgent is already a property a roll can carry.
#
# A Counter Action spent on a Clash that is then lost is spent: the entry gives nothing
# back, and the Counter Action bought the chance rather than the outcome.
#
# --- What is not built ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Exploitable line's rider. "If you are knocked through a Health Threshold due to an
# Attacking Maneuver from the Exploit Maneuver in response to this Maneuver, you
# automatically fail the Strike Roll for this Maneuver's effects."
#
# Three facts have to be joined: that a Threshold was crossed, that the attack that did it
# came through the Exploit Maneuver, and that the Exploit answered this card. The first two
# exist - Thresholds are a Moment with a pending list, and an attack carries `provokedBy`.
# The third does not: an Exploit offer knows which card provoked it, and nothing carries
# that as far as the damage. So the rider is carried as written on the Exploitable line,
# where the table reads it, and is not applied.
#
# Worth saying what it would do if it were: fail the Strike Roll of the first Clash, which
# means the Maneuver's effects do not happen - not that the Clash is lost for other
# purposes, since nothing else hangs on it.
#
# "Minions: Non-Minion" - the first Minion line in these rules that is a restriction rather
# than "Free" or "N/A": it may not be aimed at a Minion. There are no Minions in this
# system, so there is nothing to refuse.
#
# "Within your Melee Range", and the unoccupied Square. Said and not policed, like every
# distance here.
#
# --- How it is gained ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not known. No Skill's entry names it, and a Maneuver at 10(T) that turns somebody into a
# teacup for a whole Encounter reads more like a racial or a Talent than like something two
# Skill Ranks hand over. Until an entry says, it is one of the Special Maneuvers an effect
# has to grant.

# --- Cannot act while they are the Item -----------------------------------------------------
#
# The Resource is declared in this file's header, since this is the rule that hands it out,
# and it carries a clock to the end of the Combat Encounter. Read here because this file's
# passive runs on every character that owns a copy of the Maneuver, which is everybody -
# and the one being held as a teacup is not the one who used it.
[passive]
if (anitem.stacks > 0) {
    forbid maneuvers;
}
